  "description": "Given an employees table (employee_id, employee_name, department, salary), return every employee whose salary is among the top three distinct salary levels within their department. Ties at the third salary level must all be included.",
  "theory": "## What Is Top Three Earners per Department?\n\nIn scientific array computing with NumPy, vectorized tensor operations apply mathematical transformations across entire arrays without Python loop overhead.\n\nGiven an employees table (employee_id, employee_name, department, salary), return every employee whose salary is among the top three distinct salary levels within their department. Ties at the third salary level must all be included.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulation & Derivation\n\nBroadcasting rules and axis-specific reductions guarantee consistent behavior across scalars, vectors, and higher-order matrices.\n\n---\n\n## Key Properties & Conceptual Intuition\n\n- Rank salaries within each department using DENSE_RANK; order results by department, salary descending, then employee_id.\n- **Related Topics in This Track:** **Transformer Block**, **Decoupled RoPE**, **Dense Prefix Layers**.\n\n---\n\n## Numerical Stability & Edge Cases\n\n- **Boundary & Shape Consistency:** Ensure 1D vectors, 2D matrices, and single-element inputs preserve their expected output dimensions and rounding precision.\n\n---\n\n## Computational Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ is the total number of input elements, executing in a single vectorized pass.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to hold the output array (or $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ auxiliary working memory beyond the result).",
